{
  "dom_id": "step-q132179",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Given that the random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\sqrt{3}),$ calculate $P(X<\\frac{1}{3}).$ Round your final answer to four decimal places.",
  "worked_solution": "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\sqrt{3}).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(X<\\frac{1}{3})$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<\\frac{1}{3}) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/3}\\sqrt{3}{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}x}{|}_{0}^{1/3} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}/3}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.4386\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
